In this lesson, we'll look at how every real number on the number line splits into two big families, rational and irrational, and why that matters once surds show up. First up, rational numbers. A number is rational if you can write it as one integer divided by another, so long as the bottom one isn't zero. In decimal form, rational numbers are the well behaved ones. They either stop, like zero point five, or they repeat forever, like zero point three three three and so on. Next, irrational numbers. These are the rebels. You cannot write them as one integer over another, and their decimals just keep going with no repeating pattern. The classic examples are the square roots of non square integers, like root two, root three, root five, and so on. There's even a neat proof that root two is irrational, which goes like this. Assume you could write root two as a fraction in its simplest form. Squaring both sides forces both the top and the bottom to be even, which contradicts the fraction being in lowest terms. So no such fraction exists. Finally, a surd is just an irrational number kept exact using a root sign, like root five or the cube root of eleven, so we don't lose precision to decimals. Now let's see it in action.